WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.290
So let's say you're lost in the woods.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.020
First of all, what the hell, how are you lost in the woods, how do not have an emergency plan, a map,

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.920
a cell phone, something, but OK,

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:12.900
let's say you're lost in the woods, let's say
forces unknown have dropped you off here

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.770
and left you to fend for yourself. OK.
You know that

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:20.390
given you have no references from the outside world – let's say it's cloudy,

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:25.039
as it is – so you don't know where the Sun is. You know
that humans will generally wander

00:00:25.039 --> 00:00:28.449
in a very large circle around and around and around

00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:32.079
until they collapse from exhaustion. But you
know that if you keep going in one direction,

00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:35.420
if you manage to keep going straight,
then you might well survive.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:39.100
But how do you know you're keeping in one
direction without a compass or a map?

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.920
Well, you should know, this is something
that most people know, that moss

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.710
grows on the north side of trees like this

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:51.360
Because moss prefers damp conditions, dark conditions,
and in the Northern Hemisphere

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.000
the north side is going to be damper and darker
because the sun will normally shine on the south.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.460
It's opposite of course in the Southern
Hemisphere, sun's mostly in the north there,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.730
so moss will grow on the south. But I'm currently in
the Northern Hemisphere – I know that much at least –

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:07.570
so this must be the north side of the
tree.... No.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:10.930
No, actually, that's not true at all
because north

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:14.250
is that way. North side of the tree:

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:18.299
no moss. Because it's actually much more
complicated than that, which is something you

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:19.119
might not know.

00:01:19.119 --> 00:01:22.840
You should not use moss for emergency navigation,

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:27.500
because the micro climates in the area – 
particularly here, I'm in Ravenna Park in

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:32.500
Seattle, this is just a ravine with very little in the
way of sun

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.579
from any direction – the micro-climate here
means that the west side of the tree

00:01:36.579 --> 00:01:40.150
is actually going to be the darkest and dankest and mossiest.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:44.270
So if you're in a flat plane in perfect
conditions, well perhaps

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:48.159
it might help you, but for emergency
navigation your best idea...

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:51.159
Well, look, let's put it this way: I'm not Bear Grylls

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:54.729
the best way of surviving is not to get lost
in the woods in the first place.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.500
